There are three modes in which SYSKEY operates.
- Password Startup: this option prompts administrator to enter System Key password during system start up. This password is not stored locally.
- Store Startup Key on Floppy disk: this option is most secured than any other method. The computer-generated key is stored on a floppy disk instead of local hard disk. Domain controller will not boot unless you provide floppy disk when prompted during start up.
- Store Startup key Locally: machine-generated key is stored on the local system and that key is encrypted by using a complex encryption algorithm. This is default configuration of SYSKEY.

To create a system key:
1. Click Start > Run and type CMD.
2. Enter SYSKEY at the command prompt.
3. Click UPDATE. The ENCRYPTION ENABLED option should be selected.
4. To require a syskey startup password, click PASSWORD STARTUP.
5. Enter a strong password (the password can be from 12 to 128 characters).
6. If you don''t want to require a startup password, click SYSTEM GENERATED PASSWORD.
7. The default option is STORE STARTUP KEY LOCALLY. If you want to store the key on a floppy disk, select STORE STARTUP KEY ON FLOPPY DISK. store startup key on floppy or local', N'Management Practices', N'Management Practices', 1, NULL, N'Components', NULL, NULL, 683, 24, NULL, NULL)

